Stegosaurus lived about 150 million years ago in what is now the western USA (Colorado, Utah and Wyoming), in rocks called the Morrison Formation. Giant long-necked [[sauropod|sauropods]] like Diplodocus and Brachiosaurus lived there too, along with the hunter Allosaurus. Stegosaurus fossils have also been found in Portugal, which lay much closer to North America then, before the Atlantic Ocean grew wide.

It was named in 1877 by the American scientist Othniel Charles Marsh, during a bitter rivalry with another fossil hunter, called the [[@bone-wars|Bone Wars]]. The name means "roof lizard", because Marsh first thought the plates lay flat on its back, like roof tiles.

{{story:bone-wars}}

The first spacewalk nearly ended badly. On 18 March 1965, the Soviet cosmonaut Alexei Leonov floated out of his spacecraft for about 12 minutes. In the [[vacuum|vacuum]], his suit swelled up like a balloon, and he couldn't get back through the narrow hatch. He let out some air, squeezed back in and survived. It happened in the middle of the [[@space-race|space race]] between the Soviet Union and the United States.

{{story:space-race}}

In 2013 the Italian astronaut Luca Parmitano was on a spacewalk outside the [[@space-station|International Space Station]] when water began leaking into his helmet. It covered his eyes and nose, and he found his way back to the airlock mostly by feel. A clog in part of the suit's cooling system was to blame, and NASA changed the design afterwards.

The top 200 m is the busiest part of the sea. There is enough light here for tiny drifting plants called phytoplankton to make food by [[@photosynthesis|photosynthesis]]. They are so many that they make around half of the oxygen in the air you breathe.

Almost everything in the ocean depends on them. Small animals eat the phytoplankton, fish eat the small animals, and bigger hunters eat the fish. [[@coral-reefs|Coral reefs]], dolphins, sea turtles and most of the fish we eat live in this zone. It is also the only layer most people ever see. Even a strong swimmer holding their breath rarely gets below about 10 m, a tiny scratch on the surface of the sea.

{{story:photosynthesis}}

When you press the handle or button, it lifts a seal, called a flapper or a flush valve, from a hole in the bottom of the cistern. Water pours down into the bowl, pushing waste through the S-shaped bend and into the drain.

When the cistern is nearly empty, the seal falls back into place. Now the float, sitting low, has opened the fill valve. Water refills both the cistern and, through a small tube, the bowl. The bowl must keep some water to block smells coming up from the [[@toilets-sewers|sewers]].

{{story:floating-ships}}

In 1679, the French scientist Denis Papin, working in London, showed members of the Royal Society a heavy iron pot with a tightly clamped lid. He called it a "steam digester". It could soften bones until they crumbled, and he later cooked a supper in it for the society's members.

Papin also added a safety valve, a weighted [[@lever-lift|lever]] that let steam escape before the pot burst. Many inventors after him experimented with the push of steam, and similar ideas later helped shape the first [[@steam-engine|steam engines]].

{{story:steam-engine}}

Often the tune comes first. A music director might hum a melody or play it on a [[@harmonium|harmonium]]. The lyricist then writes words that fit. Sometimes it works the other way round, with a poem set to music.

In the classic era, dozens of musicians recorded together in one big room: violins, [[@flutes|flutes]], sitar, mandolin, [[@tabla|tabla]] and dholak. The singer stood at a microphone, and everyone had to get it right in one go. Today, each part is usually recorded separately on computers and mixed later. A singer may record a line dozens of times, and engineers pick the best pieces.

{{story:bollywood}}

On the night of 14–15 August 1947, India gained independence. At the Red Fort in Delhi, Bismillah Khan was invited to play the shehnai as the new nation began. He played there again for the first Republic Day in 1950.

For decades after, his shehnai was broadcast on Doordarshan on Independence Day. To many Indians, its sound became tied to national celebrations, the way [[@fireworks|fireworks]] are to the Fourth of July in the United States.

{{story:varanasi}}

People tend to treat their own habits as normal and everyone else's as strange. Scientists who study societies have a word for this: ethnocentrism, from the Greek for "nation" and "centre". It is very common and usually not meant unkindly.

In the early 1900s, the anthropologist Franz Boas, who worked in the USA, argued that cultures should be studied on their own terms rather than ranked as "advanced" or "primitive". His students carried this idea into schools and museums. It changed how many people think about other societies, although debates about it continue.

{{story:learning-languages}}

Most things in daily life grow by adding. If you add 1 cm each time, 42 steps give you 42 cm. Doubling is different. Each step adds as much as all the steps before it put together. Mathematicians call this exponential growth, and they write it using [[@exponents|exponents]]: 2 to the power 42 is about 4.4 trillion.

The same trick appears in the old legend of [[@puzzle-chessboard-rice|rice on a chessboard]], where doubling one grain 63 times needs more rice than the world grows in centuries.

{{story:apollo-moon}}

Egyptians tracked the night by watching stars rise, and the day with shadow clocks. They divided each into 12 hours. But because days are longer in summer than in winter, their hours changed length through the year. A summer daytime hour was much longer than a winter one.

Equal hours only became normal after mechanical [[@clocks|clocks]] spread across Europe in the 1300s. A gear-driven clock can't easily change speed with the [[@seasons-why|seasons]], so hours became fixed.

{{story:clocks}}

In Delhi and some other northern cities, 15 August is [[@pop-kites-patang|kite day]]. Rooftops in Old Delhi are crowded from morning, and many kites are printed in saffron, white and green. It's a cousin of the January [[@kites-festival|kite festival]] of Gujarat.

Schools across India hold flag hoistings, sing patriotic songs and hand out sweets. Buildings are lit up in the colours of the [[@flags|flag]] at night, and many families watch the Red Fort speech live on TV.

{{story:fireworks}}

Computers count using [[@binary-code|binary]], a number system with only two digits, 0 and 1. Off is 0, on is 1. [[@maths-binary-fingers|The number 5 in binary is 101]].

To add two [[binary|binary]] numbers, you need a [[circuit|circuit]] called an adder, made from a handful of logic gates. In Minecraft, players lay out redstone in neat rows to build adders, then chain many together to handle bigger numbers. A row of lamps at the end shows the answer as lit and unlit bulbs.

{{story:binary-code}}

Your phone is always sensing its surroundings. An accelerometer feels movement and tilt, which is how the screen knows to rotate and how [[@smartwatches|step counters]] work. A gyroscope senses turning. A magnetometer acts as a compass.

A proximity sensor near the top notices when you hold the phone to your ear and switches off the screen so your cheek doesn't press buttons. A light sensor adjusts the brightness. A satellite receiver listens for signals from navigation satellites, so maps can show where you are.

{{story:gps-how}}

In 1741, the Dutch tried to push into the small kingdom of Travancore in what is now southern Kerala. Its king, Marthanda Varma, met them at Colachel and defeated them.

A Dutch officer named Eustachius De Lannoy was captured. Instead of punishing him, Marthanda Varma made him a commander, and De Lannoy spent years training Travancore's army in European ways. After Colachel, Dutch power in India steadily faded. They handed their last Indian posts to the British in the early 1800s.

{{story:cardamom-pepper}}

After Shivaji died in 1680, his son Sambhaji took over. In 1689 he was captured and executed by Aurangzeb, who had moved his whole court to the Deccan to finish off the Marathas.

The Marathas did not give up. Sambhaji's brother Rajaram escaped all the way to Gingee in Tamil Nadu. When Rajaram died in 1700, his widow Tarabai led the resistance. Maratha bands raided across the Deccan, and the war dragged on for years. Aurangzeb died in 1707, still in the Deccan, without having won. The war had drained the [[@mughals|Mughal]] treasury.

{{story:western-ghats}}

At Abu Simbel in southern Egypt, giant statues of Ramesses II sit in front of a temple. In 591 BC, Greek soldiers serving an Egyptian king marched past on a campaign. Some of them carved their names and a short note into the leg of one statue.

The statues were already about 700 years old by then. To those soldiers, Ramesses was ancient history, the way the age of Ashoka feels ancient to us.

{{story:vikings}}

In 1842 Manu married Gangadhar Rao, the Raja of Jhansi, and took the name Lakshmibai. Their son was born in 1851 but died as a baby. In 1853, the ailing Raja adopted a young relative, renamed him Damodar Rao, and died the next day.

The Governor-General, Lord Dalhousie, used a rule called the Doctrine of Lapse to take over Jhansi. The Rani was given a pension and told to leave the fort. She sent petitions and hired an Australian-born lawyer, John Lang, to plead her case. The Company would not budge.

{{story:poetry}}

Monaco sits on a rocky strip of the Mediterranean coast. It is about 2 square km, about the size of a big city neighbourhood in Mumbai. About 38,000 people live there, which makes it one of the most crowded countries anywhere.

Monaco is famous for its harbour full of yachts and its Formula One race through narrow city streets. It has added land by building out into the sea. Its army is tiny, and France has agreed to help defend it.

{{story:sea-level-rise}}

In [[@texas|Texas]], Johnson Space Center is home to NASA's astronauts. They learn to fly jets, fix space station parts and survive in rough places. One of its key tools is a huge indoor pool where astronauts in spacesuits practise spacewalks underwater, which feels a bit like floating in space. Much of the [[@astronaut-training|astronaut training]] happens here. Next door, the visitor centre, Space Center Houston, lets people walk inside a full-size model of a space shuttle sitting on top of a real jumbo jet.

Mission Control is here too. Flight controllers sit at rows of screens, watching every detail of a mission. When Apollo 13's crew radioed "Houston, we've had a problem" in 1970, it was these engineers who worked out how to get them home.

{{story:mars-rovers}}

In one story, Akbar says a poor man may win a reward if he stands all night in a freezing lake. The man survives, saying he kept his eyes on a lamp burning far away in the palace. A courtier claims the lamp warmed him, so the reward is refused.

The next day, Birbal doesn't come to court. Akbar finds him sitting by a small fire, with a pot of khichdi hanging high above it. "It will cook soon," says Birbal. Akbar laughs and calls it foolish, until he sees the point. If a faraway lamp cannot cook rice, it cannot warm a man either. The poor man gets his reward.

{{story:puzzles-logic}}

Making gulab jamun takes time. First comes khoya. Milk is stirred in a heavy pan for an hour or more until most of the water steams away. Khoya is kneaded with a little flour and a pinch of baking soda, then rolled into smooth balls with no cracks. Any crack can split open in the oil.

The balls are fried in ghee or oil over low heat. They sink at first, then slowly rise and puff up as they turn brown. Rushing this step leaves a raw centre. Finally they go into warm syrup flavoured with cardamom and rosewater. Over a few hours, they soak it up like sponges and swell noticeably. Some cooks hide a few strands of saffron, a pinch of cardamom or a crystal of sugar candy inside each ball, which melts into a sweet pocket.

{{story:ancient-persia}}

Sugarcane probably first grew in New Guinea, but India was one of the first places where people learned to turn its juice into solid sugar. Ancient Indian texts mention gur and other sugars. By around the [[@gupta-golden-age|Gupta period]], about 1,500 years ago, Indians were making crystallised sugar.

{{story:words-borrowed}}

The Sanskrit word for sugar, "sharkara", travelled through Persian, Arabic and Latin into English as "sugar". The Sanskrit "khanda", meaning a piece of sugar, likely gave us "candy". These [[@words-borrowed|borrowed words]] carry the memory of India's role in the sugar story.
